1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:03,882
If Earth stopped spinning right now, you wouldn't stop with it.

2
00:00:03,882 --> 00:00:08,559
You'd keep going, east, at up to sixteen hundred and seventy kilometers an hour.

3
00:00:08,559 --> 00:00:10,196
So would the air around you.

4
00:00:10,196 --> 00:00:14,872
So would the oceans, the cars, the buildings, anything not welded to solid rock.

5
00:00:14,872 --> 00:00:16,509
And here's the strange part.

6
00:00:16,509 --> 00:00:21,128
The safest place on the whole planet would be a spot where almost nobody lives.

7
00:00:21,827 --> 00:00:24,480
So let's stop the Earth three different ways.

8
00:00:24,480 --> 00:00:25,305
In one second.

9
00:00:25,305 --> 00:00:26,425
Over a few decades.

10
00:00:26,425 --> 00:00:30,492
And the way it's actually happening, right now, while you watch this.

11
00:00:30,492 --> 00:00:38,979
By the end, you'll know where the oceans would go, why a single day would last an entire year, and why your own days are already getting longer.

12
00:00:39,680 --> 00:00:41,109
What If Earth Stopped Spinning?

13
00:00:41,809 --> 00:00:43,651
First, how fast are we moving?

14
00:00:43,651 --> 00:00:47,580
Earth turns once every twenty three hours and fifty six minutes.

15
00:00:47,580 --> 00:00:52,430
At the equator, the ground covers about forty thousand kilometers in that time.

16
00:00:52,430 --> 00:00:56,236
That's roughly sixteen hundred and seventy kilometers an hour.

17
00:00:56,236 --> 00:01:01,455
Move away from the equator and the circle you ride on gets smaller, so you go slower.

18
00:01:01,455 --> 00:01:04,095
New York, about twelve hundred and seventy.

19
00:01:04,095 --> 00:01:05,630
London, about a thousand.

20
00:01:05,630 --> 00:01:08,208
At the North Pole, you just turn in place.

21
00:01:08,208 --> 00:01:12,874
And you feel none of it, for the same reason you don't feel a smooth flight.

22
00:01:12,874 --> 00:01:14,471
Speed itself is invisible.

23
00:01:14,471 --> 00:01:16,128
Only changes in speed hurt.

24
00:01:16,828 --> 00:01:20,094
So how do we even know we're spinning, if we can't feel it?

25
00:01:20,094 --> 00:01:28,341
In eighteen fifty one, Léon Foucault hung a heavy iron ball from a sixty seven meter wire, under the dome of the Panthéon in Paris, and let it swing.

26
00:01:28,341 --> 00:01:33,101
Hour after hour, the line of the swing slowly turned, about eleven degrees every hour.

27
00:01:33,101 --> 00:01:34,651
The pendulum wasn't turning.

28
00:01:34,651 --> 00:01:37,141
The floor of Paris was turning underneath it.

29
00:01:37,141 --> 00:01:40,684
At the North Pole, the swing would turn a full circle every day.

30
00:01:40,684 --> 00:01:42,898
At the equator, it wouldn't turn at all.

31
00:01:43,598 --> 00:01:44,571
Stop number one.

32
00:01:44,571 --> 00:01:46,760
The planet halts in a single second.

33
00:01:46,760 --> 00:01:47,672
The rock stops.

34
00:01:47,672 --> 00:01:49,680
Everything resting on it doesn't.

35
00:01:49,680 --> 00:01:55,640
At the equator, you would be launched east at about four hundred and sixty five meters per second.

36
00:01:55,640 --> 00:01:57,951
That's faster than the speed of sound.

37
00:01:57,951 --> 00:02:06,040
Cars, trees, people, loose soil, roof tiles, all sliding and tumbling in the same direction, at the same moment, everywhere on Earth.

38
00:02:06,740 --> 00:02:07,974
Then the air arrives.

39
00:02:07,974 --> 00:02:15,612
The atmosphere doesn't stop either, so near the equator it becomes a wind of up to sixteen hundred and seventy kilometers an hour.

40
00:02:15,612 --> 00:02:26,658
For comparison, the strongest gust ever measured on Earth was four hundred and eight kilometers an hour, recorded on Barrow Island, Australia, during Cyclone Olivia in nineteen ninety six.

41
00:02:26,658 --> 00:02:31,594
The stop wind would be about four times faster, scouring the ground down to bedrock.

42
00:02:32,294 --> 00:02:39,979
The oceans keep moving too, piling up against every coast that faces east, as walls of water bigger than anything in recorded history.

43
00:02:39,979 --> 00:02:42,274
And all that motion has to go somewhere.

44
00:02:42,274 --> 00:02:48,181
By our calculation, the energy stored in Earth's spin is about two times ten to the twenty nine joules.

45
00:02:48,181 --> 00:02:52,254
That's as much energy as the entire Sun puts out in about nine minutes.

46
00:02:52,254 --> 00:02:58,448
Stop the planet in one second, and much of that turns into heat, through friction, crushing, and turbulence.

47
00:02:59,148 --> 00:03:00,109
Now the paradox.

48
00:03:00,109 --> 00:03:02,511
Look at Earth from above the North Pole.

49
00:03:02,511 --> 00:03:06,174
The farther from the center, the faster the ground is moving.

50
00:03:06,174 --> 00:03:09,177
But right at the poles, it's barely moving at all.

51
00:03:09,177 --> 00:03:14,521
Stand exactly on the pole, and when the planet stops, you'd simply stop turning in place.

52
00:03:14,521 --> 00:03:15,121
No launch.

53
00:03:15,121 --> 00:03:22,147
The worst second in the history of the planet, and the safest spots are two patches of ice where almost nobody lives.

54
00:03:22,147 --> 00:03:27,972
The few dozen people wintering at the South Pole research station would survive the first second.

55
00:03:27,972 --> 00:03:30,374
The global storm would reach them later.

56
00:03:31,074 --> 00:03:32,567
Stop number two is gentle.

57
00:03:32,567 --> 00:03:36,585
The spin fades over a few decades, so slowly that nothing gets thrown.

58
00:03:36,585 --> 00:03:37,561
You'd survive it.

59
00:03:37,561 --> 00:03:40,431
You just might not recognize the planet afterward.

60
00:03:40,431 --> 00:03:42,441
Because Earth isn't a perfect ball.

61
00:03:42,441 --> 00:03:44,852
Its spin makes it bulge around the middle.

62
00:03:44,852 --> 00:03:49,846
The equator sits about twenty one kilometers farther from the center than the poles do.

63
00:03:49,846 --> 00:03:51,511
And the oceans bulge with it.

64
00:03:51,511 --> 00:03:56,333
The spin holds up a hump of water around the equator, roughly eight kilometers high.

65
00:03:57,033 --> 00:03:59,691
Take the spin away, and that hump collapses.

66
00:03:59,691 --> 00:04:02,229
The water runs downhill, toward the poles.

67
00:04:02,229 --> 00:04:12,077
In a well known simulation by Witold Fraczek at the mapping company Esri, the oceans end up split into two great polar seas, one in the north and one in the south.

68
00:04:12,077 --> 00:04:19,569
And around the middle of the planet, a single giant continent rises out of the water, wrapping all the way around the Earth.

69
00:04:19,569 --> 00:04:26,034
Along its spine, Chimborazo, in Ecuador, would tower more than thirteen kilometers above the new sea level.

70
00:04:26,034 --> 00:04:26,517
And you?

71
00:04:26,517 --> 00:04:35,640
Standing on the equator, you'd weigh about a third of a percent more, because the spin that used to fling you outward, ever so slightly, would be gone.

72
00:04:36,340 --> 00:04:37,664
Then there's the Sun.

73
00:04:37,664 --> 00:04:41,007
Today, a day lasts twenty four hours because we spin.

74
00:04:41,007 --> 00:04:47,502
Stop the spin, and the only thing that changes which side of Earth faces the Sun is our trip around it.

75
00:04:47,502 --> 00:04:49,646
One day would last one whole year.

76
00:04:49,646 --> 00:04:53,556
About six months of daylight, followed by six months of night.

77
00:04:54,256 --> 00:05:00,305
The day side would bake under a Sun that never sets, while the night side freezes through months of darkness.

78
00:05:00,305 --> 00:05:02,858
And the weather would be rebuilt from scratch.

79
00:05:02,858 --> 00:05:05,910
Today, Earth's spin is what twists storms into spirals.

80
00:05:05,910 --> 00:05:12,458
It's why hurricanes almost never form within about five degrees of the equator, where that twisting effect fades away.

81
00:05:12,458 --> 00:05:15,455
On a still Earth, there would be no hurricanes at all.

82
00:05:15,455 --> 00:05:20,283
Instead, air would flow in giant loops, from the hot day side to the frozen night side.

83
00:05:20,983 --> 00:05:24,673
And there's one more risk that scientists aren't sure about.

84
00:05:24,673 --> 00:05:32,362
Earth's magnetic field comes from molten iron churning in the outer core, and the planet's spin helps organize that churning.

85
00:05:32,362 --> 00:05:40,112
Venus is almost the same size as Earth, but it turns so slowly that one rotation takes two hundred and forty three Earth days.

86
00:05:40,112 --> 00:05:42,264
That's longer than its entire year.

87
00:05:42,264 --> 00:05:45,340
And Venus has no global magnetic field of its own.

88
00:05:45,340 --> 00:05:47,677
Slow spin may not be the whole reason.

89
00:05:47,677 --> 00:05:53,766
But without a magnetic shield, the solar wind would have a much easier time stripping away our air.

90
00:05:54,466 --> 00:05:56,738
Which brings us to stop number three.

91
00:05:56,738 --> 00:05:57,536
The real one.

92
00:05:57,536 --> 00:05:59,500
Earth is slowing down right now.

93
00:05:59,500 --> 00:06:04,902
The Moon's gravity raises tides, and the tides drag on the spinning planet like a brake.

94
00:06:04,902 --> 00:06:06,375
Days used to be shorter.

95
00:06:06,375 --> 00:06:16,811
A seventy million year old fossil clam, studied in twenty twenty, laid down daily growth rings showing that a year back then had about three hundred and seventy two days.

96
00:06:16,811 --> 00:06:20,004
Each day lasted about twenty three and a half hours.

97
00:06:20,004 --> 00:06:26,695
And ancient rock layers suggest that one point four billion years ago, a day lasted just over eighteen hours.

98
00:06:27,395 --> 00:06:33,757
The same tidal brake is pushing the Moon away from us, by about three point eight centimeters every year.

99
00:06:33,757 --> 00:06:36,666
That's roughly as fast as your fingernails grow.

100
00:06:36,666 --> 00:06:37,817
It's all very slow.

101
00:06:37,817 --> 00:06:43,331
Today, our days are getting longer by only a couple of thousandths of a second per century.

102
00:06:44,031 --> 00:06:45,763
And it isn't perfectly smooth.

103
00:06:45,763 --> 00:06:52,691
Since nineteen seventy two, timekeepers have added twenty seven leap seconds to keep our clocks in step with the planet.

104
00:06:52,691 --> 00:06:55,982
Lately, Earth has actually been spinning slightly faster.

105
00:06:55,982 --> 00:07:08,223
A twenty twenty four study in Nature found that melting polar ice is slowing the spin back down, just enough to delay what could become the first negative leap second ever, now expected around twenty twenty nine.

106
00:07:08,923 --> 00:07:10,754
So will Earth ever actually stop?

107
00:07:10,754 --> 00:07:12,640
The Moon already lost this battle.

108
00:07:12,640 --> 00:07:17,745
Earth's tides braked its spin long ago, which is why the Moon always shows us the same face.

109
00:07:17,745 --> 00:07:20,963
But for Earth, it won't happen in any future that matters.

110
00:07:20,963 --> 00:07:26,900
For the Moon to brake us until one face of Earth always points at it, would take tens of billions of years.

111
00:07:26,900 --> 00:07:31,283
Long before that, in about five billion years, the Sun swells into a red giant.

112
00:07:31,283 --> 00:07:34,502
Earth will still be turning when the Sun runs out of time.

113
00:07:35,202 --> 00:07:37,292
So, what if Earth stopped spinning?

114
00:07:37,292 --> 00:07:43,386
In one second, it's the worst day in the planet's history, unless you happen to be standing on a pole.

115
00:07:43,386 --> 00:07:48,045
Over a few decades, the oceans run to the poles and a single day lasts a year.

116
00:07:48,045 --> 00:07:52,107
And in reality, it's happening at the speed of a growing fingernail.

117
00:07:52,107 --> 00:07:53,780
Our planet turns once a day.

118
00:07:53,780 --> 00:07:58,679
But out there are dead stars that spin more than seven hundred times every second.

119
00:07:58,679 --> 00:08:03,517
They're so dense that a single teaspoon of one would weigh as much as a mountain.

120
00:08:03,517 --> 00:08:05,071
How is that even possible?

121
00:08:05,071 --> 00:08:05,788
That's next.
